<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aphra Behn</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1640">1640</a>–<a href="/years/1689">1689</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aphra Behn (1640–1689) was an English playwright, poet and novelist, one of the first Englishwomen to earn a living by writing. She worked as a spy for Charles II in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-anglo-dutch-war">Second Anglo-Dutch War</a> and later wrote many successful plays, such as The Rover. Her short novel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oroonoko">Oroonoko</a> (1688), about an enslaved African prince in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname">Suriname</a>, draws on her claimed stay in the English colony there in the 1660s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aphra-behn.jpg" alt="Aphra Behn" width="320" height="382" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Peter Lely · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peter_Lely_-_Aphra_Behn_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The writer <b>Aphra Behn</b> claimed to have lived there and set her novel Oroonoko in the colony.</blockquote><a href="/state/willoughbyland">Willoughbyland</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.28,1.08&amp;year=1671" aria-label="Canterbury on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4574 387 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Aphra Behn"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5024 657h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5024 657h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.28,1.08&amp;year=1671">What was here in 1671? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;It may be easily guess’d, in what Manner the Prince resented this Indignity, who may be best resembled to a Lion taken in a Toil; so he raged, so he struggled for Liberty, but all in vain…&#8221;</p><footer>Oroonoko, or The Royal Slave (1688) &#183; original &#183; the narrator, on the prince seized by an English slave captain &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/29854/pg29854-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…if they died in the Attempt, it would be more brave, than to live in perpetual Slavery.&#8221;</p><footer>Oroonoko, or The Royal Slave (1688) &#183; original &#183; the narrator reporting Oroonoko's speech to his fellow captives &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/29854/pg29854-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I do not pretend, in giving you the History of this ROYAL SLAVE, to entertain my Reader with the Adventures of a feign…&#8221;</p><footer>Oroonoko, or The Royal Slave (1688), opening &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/29854/pg29854-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 539 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
